+5.  **Initialization:** Initialize Gaussian centers $\mu^{init}_i$ using the points in $P$ (i.e., $\mu^{init}_i = p_i \in P$) [S2].
+6.  **Geometry Prediction (Training):**
+    *   For each initialized center $\mu^{init}_i$, query the feature volume $F$ using trilinear interpolation at the updated location $\mu^{init}_i + \Delta \mu^{prev}_i$ to obtain a feature vector $f_i$:
+        $$ f_i = F(\mu^{init}_i + \Delta \mu^{prev}_i) $$
+        where $\Delta \mu^{prev}_i$ is the position offset predicted in the previous iteration (initialized to 0 for the first iteration) [S2].
+    *   Decode opacity and covariance using the MLP heads:
+        $$ \alpha_i = \mathcal{D}_{opa}(f_i), \quad \Sigma_i = \mathcal{D}_{cov}(f_i) $$
+    *   Decode the position offset $\Delta \mu_i$:
+        $$ \Delta \mu_i = \text{Tanh}(\mathcal{D}_{pos}(f_i)) \cdot v_{\text{size}} $$
+        where $v_{\text{size}}$ is the voxel size scaling factor [S2].
+    *   Update the Gaussian mean:
+        $$ \mu_i = \mu^{init}_i + \Delta \mu_i $$
+    *   Track $\Delta \mu_i$ as $\Delta \mu^{prev}_i$ for the next iteration [S2].
+7.  **Geometry Prediction (Inference):** Initialize $\Delta \mu^{prev}_i = 0$. Recursively execute the update rules for $\Delta \mu_i$, $\alpha_i$, and $\Sigma_i$ twice to obtain a converged $\Delta \mu_i$. Use the converged point to query $f_i$ and decode $\alpha_i$ and $\Sigma_i$ [S2].
+8.  **Color Prediction:** Project the 3D Gaussians to the nearest $K$ reference views to retrieve 2D context (color windows $\{c_k\}_{k=1}^K$ and visibility maps). Decode the Gaussian color $c_i$ using this 2D context [S2].
+9.  **Background Modeling:** Model distant views and the sky with a generalizable hemisphere Gaussian model where geometry is fixed and color is predicted similarly to the foreground [S2].
+10. **Rendering:** Compose the foreground and background Gaussians to reconstruct the image using standard alpha blending:
+    $$ c = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} c_i \alpha_i \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j) $$
+    where $c$ is the rendered color, $\mathcal{G}$ is the set of Gaussians, and the product term represents transmittance [S2].
 
 **Parameters:**
-- $N$: Number of input images [S2].
-- $N_p$: Number of points in the global point cloud [S2].
-- $K$: Number of nearest reference views for color retrieval [S2].
-- $v_{size}$: Voxel size used to scale the position offset output [S2].
+*   $N$: Number of input images [S2].
+*   $N_p$: Number of primitives in the global point cloud [S2].
+*   $K$: Number of nearest reference views for color retrieval [S2].
+*   $v_{\text{size}}$: Voxel size used to scale the position offset [S2].
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Ghosting Artifacts:** Pixel-aligned methods generate redundant and inconsistent primitives in overlapping regions. This method addresses this by using a global volume and filtering duplicates [S2].
-- **Depth Noise:** Monocular depth predictions contain noise and floating artifacts. Statistical filtering is required to remove floaters after uniform filtering [S2].
-- **Convergence:** The recursive formulation of $\Delta \mu_i$ relies on convergence. Empirically, two iterations are sufficient during inference [S2].
